  "query_preview": "..\nKey Message 1...Rounds of showers and scattered thunderstorms are\nexpected through Wednesday, with localized heavy rainfall/flooding\npossible across parts of the Finger Lakes/North Country into \ntonight.\n\nLatest surface analysis shows a broad surface low/cold front \nextending from southern Ontario southwestward across far western New \nYork and western Pennsylvania. This system will continue to slowly \nwobble its way eastward and reach an upper Saint Lawrence Valley-\ncentral New York axis by early this evening...before devolving into \na remnant inverted trough that will slowly drift across eastern New \nYork tonight and Wednesday as secondary cyclogenesis takes place off \nthe mid-Atlantic coastline. Overall...the guidance suite has trended \na little faster and further east with the timing and the positioning \nof this system over the last 24 hours. \n\nShower coverage has decreased significantly this evening, with just \na few widely scattered showers left across the Southern Tier, Finger \nLakes, and east of Lake Ontario. While coverage as decreased, there \nwill still be a few brief/local downpours. \n\nAs the surface low/front drifts across central eastern New York and \nweakens tonight the axis of deepest moisture will be shunted off to \nour east...and this in tandem with the loss of heating will bring an \nend to the potential for heavy rainfall along with a general \ndecrease in shower potential/coverage. This being said...these will \nstill not end entirely due to the presence of lingering wraparound \nmoisture and a cyclonic northerly upslope flow that should help to \nkeep at least some scattered showers in place...particularly for \nareas east of the Genesee Valley. Meanwhile...the driest overall \nconditions will be found across northern/western portions of the \nNiagara Frontier. \n\nOn Wednesday the remnant inverted trough will linger across eastern \nNew York...while aloft the core of the supporting upper level low \nwill slowly meander its way directly across New York State. Diurnal \nheating underneath the upper low's attendant cool pool will result \nin leftover wraparound/upslope showers from the nighttime hours \ntransitioning to more diurnally-driven showers and a few isolated \nthunderstorms as we push through the day...particularly from the \nGenesee Valley/western Finger Lakes eastward where better/deeper \nmoisture will be in place. Meanwhile across far western New York \nrelatively drier air should help to keep shower coverage \nconsiderably more sparse...with mainly dry weather tending to \nprevail much of the time.  \n\nKey Message 2...Increasingly drier conditions return for the end of \nthe work week. \n\nWednesday night through Friday the upper level low and surface wave \nwill make their way northeastward into the Canadian Maritimes... \nwhile ridging at the surface and aloft settles across the central \nand eastern Great Lakes. While lingering moisture and renewed \ndiurnal heating may still be enough to support some scattered \nshowers and possibly an isolated thunderstorm or two across the \nNorth Country and interior portions of the Finger Lakes on \nThursday...by Friday the aforementioned ridging and associated \ndrying should be sufficient for a dry day areawide. With the \ndeparture of the upper low and its associated cool pool...high temps \nshould also recover to seasonable levels (upper 70s to lower 80s) \nareawide by the end of the work week.",
